<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  TAKE EXTENTS AND resize-clip — THE CLAMP AND THE EMPTY-REFUSAL
  (plan 53 §4.1 / A0b, QBX-178).

  A0a made the write-through CLAMP extents rather than overwrite them, and
  clamped a would-be-empty take to one frame with a log, because the MODEL must
  keep invariant 1′ true whatever a caller does. Refusing needs a verb that can
  REJECT, and that is `resize-clip` — this row's. The two are not alternatives:
  the clamp is the model's floor, the refusal is the edit surface's answer, and
  a direct model write still gets the clamp.

  THE SHAPE. One 4 s column over test_sawtooth.wav:
    take 0 — FULL extent [0, 192000)
    take 1 — PARTIAL [48000, 96000), ENDING EARLY on purpose
  Take 1 ending before the column does is the whole fixture: it is what tells
  "clamp the takes that reach the end" apart from "set every take's length",
  which agree on every column whose takes all reach the end.

  ONE — A RIGHT-EDGE TRIM CLAMPS, AND ONLY WHAT IT MUST. Trimming the column
  to 3 s pulls take 0 back to [0, 144000) — it reached the old end, so it
  follows — and leaves take 1 at [48000, 96000) untouched, because the user put
  that edge there and a trim it does not reach has no business moving it.

  TWO — AN EXTEND GROWS ONLY THE CLAMPED TAKE. Back out to 4 s, take 0 grows
  again (it ended at the new-old end) and take 1 STAYS at [48000, 96000). An
  extend that grew every take would silently undo a comp: take 1's edge is a
  comp decision, not a consequence of the column's length.

  THREE — A TRIM PAST A TAKE'S START IS REFUSED, AND SAID OUT LOUD. Trimming
  to 24000 would leave take 1 (starting at 48000) with no material at all.
  `assert-log` is what gates it rather than the action merely failing: a resize
  can fail for several reasons, so a case that asserted only failure would pass
  on an accident. The column must be EXACTLY as it was afterwards — a refusal
  that half-applied would be worse than one that did not refuse.

  MEASURED ON THE BASELINE (main at a3aa1472, i.e. with A0a's model but not
  A0b):
    - step ONE reads take 1 as `48000-192000` and `assert-clip-window take="1"
      duration=` reads 144000 for 48000 — `applyWindowAll` clamped the extent
      correctly and then the LEFT-EDGE LOOP wrote the column's length straight
      back over it, which is the hand-off A0a recorded at that line;
    - step TWO reads `48000-240000`, a take reaching 48000 frames PAST the end
      of its own column;
    - step THREE is NOT REFUSED at all. It applies: `assert-log` finds 0
      records containing "resize-clip: REFUSED", the column becomes 24000, and
      take 1 comes out at `23999-47999` — clamped to ONE FRAME by A0a's model
      floor, which is exactly the floor doing its job in the absence of the
      refusal this row adds. The render then reads 0.0236 and 0 where 0.067 and
      0.405 are expected.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="take_extents_resize">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- take 1 deliberately ends before the column does. -->
    <add-take clip="0,0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" startOffset="0"
              index="-1" activate="0" at="48000" length="48000"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="direct" takes="2" activeTake="0"
                        extents="0-192000;48000-96000" duration="192000"/>

    <!-- ONE: trim the column's right edge to 3 s. -->
    <resize-clip clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="144000" srcStart="0"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" duration="144000"
                        extents="0-144000;48000-96000"/>
    <!-- ...and through an attribute `main` also understands: the ACTIVE take's
         window is what assert-clip-window's duration reads. -->
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" take="0" duration="144000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" take="1" duration="48000"/>

    <!-- TWO: extend back out to 4 s. Only take 0 grows. -->
    <resize-clip clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="192000" srcStart="0"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" duration="192000"
                        extents="0-192000;48000-96000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" take="1" duration="48000"/>

    <!-- THREE: a trim that would empty take 1 is REFUSED and ANNOUNCED. -->
    <resize-clip clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="24000" srcStart="0"
                 expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-log contains="resize-clip: REFUSED" minCount="1"/>
    <!-- Nothing moved. -->
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" duration="192000"
                        extents="0-192000;48000-96000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" take="0" duration="192000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" take="1" duration="48000"/>

    <!-- The column still plays what it did: take 0 across the whole 4 s. -->
    <render filename="ter_after.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="ter_after.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.05" maxRms="0.085"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="ter_after.wav"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.37" maxRms="0.44"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
